WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.900
Who owns our VPN box, and is it patched?

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.420
Four questions decide
whether it is the way in.

00:00:07.683 --> 00:00:09.053
First, who owns it?

00:00:09.318 --> 00:00:11.708
Servers have an owner and a patch cycle.

00:00:11.928 --> 00:00:16.468
The VPN or firewall was often
installed by a supplier years ago,

00:00:16.468 --> 00:00:18.398
and sits outside both.

00:00:18.688 --> 00:00:24.348
Mandiant's 2026 report finds exploits
were the most common way in for the sixth

00:00:24.348 --> 00:00:25.238
year running.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.940
Second, how fast is it patched?

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.390
Attackers start within days,
sometimes before the fix exists.

00:00:33.805 --> 00:00:34.105
In 2025,

00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:38.685
Dutch organisations were breached through
a Citrix flaw seven weeks before the

00:00:38.685 --> 00:00:39.175
patch.

00:00:39.515 --> 00:00:44.575
Verizon finds only 26% of
known-exploited holes were fully fixed,

00:00:44.575 --> 00:00:47.405
after a median of 43 days.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.290
A box past end-of-life never gets a fix.

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:52.973
Third, what does it expose?

00:00:53.423 --> 00:00:56.993
The admin page is often reachable
from the whole internet.

00:00:57.318 --> 00:01:01.668
A VPN login with only a password
is one guess from the inside.

00:01:02.138 --> 00:01:04.188
Both are settings, not purchases.

00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:07.887
Fourth, how much does one login open?

00:01:08.172 --> 00:01:12.482
A classic VPN puts a connected
laptop inside the network.

00:01:13.327 --> 00:01:14.817
A stolen login reaches everything.

00:01:15.157 --> 00:01:16.207
With zero trust,

00:01:16.597 --> 00:01:18.547
access is granted per application,

00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:21.147
so a stolen login reaches one.

00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:23.327
What good looks like.

00:01:23.612 --> 00:01:29.292
The Five Eyes guidance of February 2025
names an inventory of internet-facing

00:01:29.292 --> 00:01:32.862
devices, with support dates,
as a baseline.

00:01:33.232 --> 00:01:36.142
Edge advisories get an
emergency patch lane.

00:01:36.477 --> 00:01:38.667
The admin page stays off the internet,

00:01:39.037 --> 00:01:41.727
and every remote login uses MFA.

00:01:42.297 --> 00:01:47.847
NIS2 Article 21 requires vulnerability
handling in the maintenance of systems.

00:01:49.797 --> 00:01:51.007
A suggested first step.

00:01:51.292 --> 00:01:54.322
Make one list of every
device facing the internet.

00:01:54.637 --> 00:01:58.667
Add an owner's name, the version,
and the vendor's end-of-support date.

00:01:58.992 --> 00:02:02.662
Then ask how many days pass
between an advisory and our patch.

00:02:04.283 --> 00:02:06.073
A list, a setting and a date.

00:02:06.388 --> 00:02:08.038
That closes most of the door.

